{#
  T-4.03 — the «Winter Is Coming» carousel.

  | Variable              | Type      | Description                                                      |
  |-----------------------|-----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | products.source       | string    |                                                                  |
  | products.source_value | ?int[]    | ids of sources (product_ids, categories_ids, brands_id..etc)     |
  | title                 | string    | Section title                                                    |
  | sub_title             | ?string   | Optional merchant subtitle                                       |
  | display_all_url       | ?string   | If it exists, show the «عرض الكل» link to this url               |
  | position              | Int       | Sorting number start from zero                                   |
  | limit                 | int       | Number of products to be shown                                   |

  WHY `salla-products-list` AND NOT `salla-products-slider`. The acceptance
  criterion is "native scroll-snap, not a JS carousel library", and
  `salla-products-slider` is swiper — a JS carousel library. But the products
  cannot be rendered in Twig either: `products.source` and `products.source_value`
  are a **descriptor**, not an array, and this template never receives the
  products themselves. They are fetched client-side.

  So the split is: `salla-products-list` fetches and renders the cards, exactly as
  it does for the fixed-products block, and **the scrolling is pure CSS** on the
  `.s-products-list-wrapper` it produces. Nothing here drives a transform, listens
  for a drag, or animates a slide. That is technique C — the platform keeps owning
  what a product list is, and only its presentation changes.

  KEYBOARD SCROLLING IS NOT A `tabindex` ON THE TRACK. Every card contains a link,
  so tabbing through the cards scrolls them into view in every browser, and the
  scroll container is operable without adding a tab stop in front of the row that
  a keyboard user would have to pass through on every visit.
#}
<section data-testid="store-home-products-slider"
         id="products-carousel-{{ position }}"
         class="s-block product-carousel container">
    {% if products|length %}
        {% include 'components.ui.section-header' with {
            title: title,
            url: display_all_url,
            level: 2
        } %}

        {# Upstream passed `block-subTitle` to the slider component. The design
           draws no subtitle, but it is merchant-authored copy, so it renders
           under the header rather than being dropped on the floor. #}
        {% if sub_title %}
            <p class="product-carousel__subtitle">{{ sub_title|raw }}</p>
        {% endif %}

        <salla-products-list
                data-testid="store-home-products-slider-widget"
                class="product-carousel__list"
                source="{{ products.source }}"
                limit="{{ limit }}"
                source-value="{{ products.source_value|json_encode }}">
        </salla-products-list>

        {#
          The scroll indicator.

          Decorative in the strict sense: it reports a position the scrollbar
          already reports, to people who can see it. `aria-hidden` because
          announcing "37% scrolled" is noise, and because the content it refers to
          is fully reachable without it. `home.js` sizes and moves the thumb, and
          hides the whole thing when there is nothing to scroll.
        #}
        <div class="product-carousel__indicator" data-carousel-indicator aria-hidden="true" hidden>
            <span class="product-carousel__thumb"></span>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</section>
